  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1259 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA consensus of ensemble and deterministic model continue to \ndepict the axis of expansive mid to upper level ridging sliding \ndirectly over Texas during the upcoming weekend as 500mb heights \nand temperatures increase. 12z LREF guidance indicates the \nstrength of the high pressure aloft will indeed be anomalous as \n500mb heights are forecast to near the 99th-100th percentile for \nthis time of year based on climatology. With warm temperatures \naloft (-4C to -5C) and a lack of synoptic forcing, our weather \nwill primarily be driven by mesoscale circulations on Saturday as \na few isolated showers and storms cannot be ruled out closer to \nwhere the greatest instability and low level moisture resides; the\nmiddle Texas coast. By Sunday, model guidance hints at the \narrival of a short wave across northern Texas out ahead of the \nnext lobe of a mid-level trough propagating eastward via the \nsouthern jet- stream. This will act to erode the mid to upper \nlevel ridge in place and veer mid to upper level flow to a \nsouthwesterly direction resulting in a slight moistening of the \nmid- level of the atmosphere. At the surface, a frontal boundary \nis forecast to slide southward into northern Texas and become \nstationary during the first part of next week. Increasing moisture\npooling to the south of the boundary and an active southern \nbranch of the jet- stream just to the north of our area could \nresult in diurnally driven isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms each day through mid-week. High temperatures are \nforecast to remain relatively consistent through Wednesday, \nresiding in the middle to upper 90s across most of the area with \nthe exception of the low 90s across the Edwards Plateau. Peak \napparent temperatures are forecast to reach the 100-105 range each\nafternoon.",
